  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Latest surface analysis this hour shows stalled\nfrontal boundary draped from roughly Wilmington northward to\nalong the I-95 corridor with high pressure centered off the \ncoast of New England extending along the mid- Atlantic coast. \nThe boundary is forecast to gradually lift north and eastward \nlater this afternoon in response to a mid-level shortwave \nforecast to track across the mid-Atlantic. Ongoing convection\nhas likely abated peak heating and will keep highs at or\nslightly below 90, capping heat indices at 100-105.\n\nTomorrow is a different story with the frontal boundary fully \nlifted into the mid-Atlantic and thicknesses soaring back to \n1435-1440+ m, setting the stage for highs in the mid to upper \n90s inland assuming relatively clear skies to start the day. The\nlevel of humidity is the main point of uncertainty as surface \nwinds will be WNW to NW, suggesting downsloping and Tds trending\nlower than model consensus. If this does occur, HI values will \nalmost certainly still reach between 105-110 F. For that reason,\nissued a Heat Advisory for tomorrow across all inland zones. A \nshort-fused upgrade to Extreme Heat Warnings, however is not out\nof the question overnight mainly along the southern coast where\nthe sea breeze will boost dew points.\n\nAnother frontal boundary quickly pushes across NC tomorrow \nevening before stalling along the coast. Like this weekend, \nsouthern counties will likely remain hot and humid while \nAlbemarle and vicinity see some modest relief. A more widespread\ncooldown, however, is on the horizon late week as a stronger \ncold front dives out of Canada and across the eastern CONUS.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Regional radar this evening shows clusters of \nstrong to severe thunderstorms ongoing from West Virginia into \nVirginia. The airmass downstream of these clusters still appears\nat least modestly supportive of severe thunderstorms. However, \nas we move further into the night, a stabilizing boundary layer \nshould lead to a lowering severe risk as the convection \napproaches SE Virginia and NE North Carolina. The forecast will \nremain unchanged through the night, but the key message for the \nrest of the night is a reduced risk of severe weather.\n\nA cold front then approaches from the north and west tomorrow \nevening. Aforementioned heat and humidity will easily boost \ninstability with a forecast 1500-2500 J/kg of MLCAPE, but shear \nwill once again be lacking at generally 20 kt or less. However, \nwith PWATs remaining at or above 2\" conditions will once again \nbe favorable for a stronger storm or two to impact the area \nbringing a damaging wind, frequent lightning, and torrential \nrain threat. Convective evolution will not be terribly different\nfrom the past couple of days, with an afternoon focus along sea\nand sound breezes and then upstream convection along remnant \ndifferential heating boundaries inland, and then an evening \nfocus as a potentially more organized complex pushes across the \nstate through the evening hours - this, like tonight, would pose\nthe higher severe threat with damaging winds. AI NWP guidance \nhas elevated probabilities, which are plausible since northwest \nflow events tend to overperform across eastern NC.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A brief return to more climo PoPs is forecast \nTue and Wed with the seabreeze and stalled front offshore being \nthe main focus for showers and thunderstorms. Stronger front is\nlikely to approach the region and stall late week, serving to\nboth offer a reprieve from oppressive heat and humidity, and a\nchance of strong to severe thunderstorms as shear increases\nalongside the boundary with stronger troughing. Given how far \nout this is, there are still several details to hammer out but \nwill be monitoring trends in the forecast for any adjustments in\ntiming and strength of this front in the coming days.",
